<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lazare Hoche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lazare Hoche was a French Revolutionary general who rose from the ranks to command an army at the age of 25. He defeated the Austrians and Prussians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> in 1793, crushed the émigré landing at Quiberon in 1795, pacified the Vendée and led the failed expedition to Ireland in 1796. As commander of the Army of Sambre-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> he encouraged a Cisrhenian Republic before dying of illness in September 1797.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lazare-hoche.jpg" alt="Lazare Hoche" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Louis Laneuville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lazare_Hoche,_1801.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lazare Hoche was born on 24 June 1768 at Montreuil, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, the son of a groom at the royal hunting grounds. His mother died when he was two, and an aunt who sold fruit mostly raised him, while his maternal uncle, the parish priest of Saint-Germain-en-Laye, educated him and made him a choirboy. In October 1784 he enlisted as a fusilier in the French Guards Regiment. He was a corporal by May 1789 and joined the National Guard that September. During the October Days he was among the guardsmen who escorted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> and his family out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Commissioned in 1792, Hoche served at Thionville and Namur and at Neerwinden in March 1793. After defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> he was promoted to general of division in October 1793 and given command of the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>. On 22 December he won at Froeschwiller, and on 26 December 1793 he drove Wurmser's Austrians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> at the Second Battle of Wissembourg. In March 1794 he was arrested on charges of treason brought by Pichegru and was held until 4 August, after the fall of Robespierre.</p><p>Given command in the west, Hoche defeated the Armée des Émigrés, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> had landed, at Fort Penthièvre on 21 July 1795, and by 1796 his mobile columns had brought about the capture of the rebel leaders Stofflet and Charette. He then led the expedition to Ireland, which sailed from Brest with between 12,000 and 25,000 troops on 15 December 1796. A gale separated his frigate from the rest, and the fleet dispersed after a failed landing attempt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantry-bay">Bantry Bay</a>. On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> he defeated the Austrians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuwied">Neuwied</a> on 18 April 1797 and in July became Minister of War, but he resigned within a month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 2 September 1797 Hoche took command of the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>. His health failed quickly, and he died of consumption at Wetzlar on 19 September, aged 29. The belief spread that he had been poisoned, but the suspicion seems unfounded. He was buried next to his friend François Marceau at Fort Petersberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, and a funeral procession was held on the Champ de Mars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 1 October. In 1919 the French Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> reburied his remains in the Monument General Hoche at Weißenthurm.</p><p>The Irish revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theobald-wolfe-tone">Wolfe Tone</a> recorded Hoche's view that guillotining a man gets rid of one individual but makes all his friends enemies of the government for ever. Statues of Hoche stand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and at Quiberon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lazare Hoche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After French armies occupied the left bank of the Rhine, local supporters of the Revolution in towns such as Bonn, Koblenz, Cologne and Aachen proclaimed a Cisrhenian Republic in September 1797, with the encouragement of General <b>Lazare Hoche</b>, who favoured a separate state allied to France.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisrhenian-republic">Cisrhenian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Versailles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Wetzlar, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.81,2.14&amp;year=1786" aria-label="Versailles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lazare Hoche"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5120 5949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5120 5949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.81,2.14&amp;year=1786">What was here in 1786? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
